{
"Deep sleep is one stage of sleep.": "931bbeb2fb30",
"Growth hormone helps children grow.": "c480fe5e8a99",
"How can light from a screen keep you awake?": "1f545832b1dc",
"How does your body know when to sleep? You have a body clock deep inside your brain, and it is set by light. When your eyes see daylight, the clock knows it is day. In the evening, as it gets dark, your brain starts to release a hormone called melatonin, which tells the body that night has come. Bright light late in the evening, including the light from screens, can stop your brain from releasing melatonin. Then you do not feel sleepy, even when your body needs rest.": "36ea6eb140b3",
"How much sleep do you need? More than adults do. Sleep doctors say children from 6 to 12 years old need 9 to 12 hours every night, and teenagers need 8 to 10. You can help your body clock. Go to bed and get up at the same time every day, even on weekends. Turn off screens at least 30 minutes before bedtime. Keep your room dark, quiet, and cool, and skip tea and cola in the evening. Now use the sleep calculator below the model: how many hours did you sleep last night?": "18cf7c3bd91f",
"I always yawn when I am tired.": "f8cfb390e26a",
"I had a funny dream about a flying bus.": "c5c6a4b4be96",
"I was still awake at midnight.": "02f2a71fa3a5",
"My bedtime is nine o'clock.": "a1a6d888a3c5",
"Sleep comes in stages. First comes light sleep: your muscles relax, and your heartbeat and breathing slow down. Next comes deep sleep, when your brain waves turn big and slow and it is very hard to wake you. Then comes REM sleep. REM stands for rapid eye movement, because your eyes dart around under your eyelids. In REM sleep your brain is almost as busy as when you are awake, and you have your most vivid dreams. Your big muscles are switched off, so you do not act your dreams out. Scientists in Chicago first described REM sleep in 1953.": "663dc4a97c4b",
"Sleep helps your memory.": "9771e707bc1d",
"The baby fell asleep in the car.": "e05b230925ec",
"The stages repeat in cycles, and in an adult one cycle takes about an hour and a half. You go through several cycles each night, but they are not all the same. Most of your deep sleep comes in the first few hours. The REM periods start short and grow longer toward morning. So if you go to bed too late, you lose deep sleep, and if you get up too early, you lose dreams.": "635347382b6c",
"What happens in your brain and body during REM sleep?": "13125c80e7ee",
"What is all this for? One job is memory. Sleep helps your brain turn what you learned today into long-term memories. That is why a good night's sleep before a test helps more than staying up late to study. Another job is growing. Your body releases growth hormone during deep sleep, which helps you grow and helps your body repair itself. Sleep also helps you fight germs: people who do not sleep enough are more likely to catch colds.": "21533d0c0e5f",
"Why do you lose deep sleep if you go to bed too late?": "06d6845c765c",
"You will spend about a third of your life asleep. It can seem like a waste of time: you lie still, your eyes are closed, and nothing happens. But sleep is not like switching off a light. Your brain stays busy all night, and your body uses the quiet hours to do jobs it cannot do well while you are running around.": "f23c493daa43",
